{"input":"When I'm cutting, should I eat more protein than when I'm bulking?","metadata":{"language":"en","retrieved":["PMC3879660::discussion::017","PMC8978023::results::018","PMC5596471::conclusion::047","PMC5477153::body::025","PMC8978023::abstract::000","PMC8978023::discussion::029","PMC5596471::body::019","PMC5214805::methods::005","PMC8978023::results::021","PMC5477153::body::013","PMC5477153::body::016","PMC8978023::discussion::023","PMC5867436::conclusion::029","PMC3879660::discussion::018","PMC8978023::discussion::025","PMC5828430::conclusion::011","PMC5828430::body::002","PMC5214805::introduction::003","PMC5477153::body::040","PMC5214805::conclusion::026"],"status":"answered","timings":{"generate":0.004662100000132341,"query_analyze":1.8000000636675395e-06,"retrieve":0.6641324000001987}},"output":"When combined with a resistance-training program and a hypoenergetic diet (cutting), an elevated daily intake of protein (2 – 3× the RDA) can promote greater losses of fat mass and greater overall improvements in body composition. [10]\nTotal daily protein intake for the goal of maximizing resistance training-induced gains in muscle mass and strength is approximately 1.6 g/kg, at least in non-dieting (eucaloric or hypercaloric) conditions. [16]\nProtein supplementation beyond a total daily protein intake of ~1.6 g/kg/day during resistance exercise training (RET) provided no further benefit on gains in muscle mass or strength. [13]","timestamp":"2026-10-20T09:50:02.068+00:00","trace_id":"f872a1dc-cfdb-4193-b3f2-dcaf9205d623"}
